<h3>P3 README:</h3>

<div>
    <p>
        This update doesn't add any new features - instead it implements Blazor components on the pages that have been built so far. I will go page by page and list the components added, as well as what they do and how state flows.       
    </p>
    <p>
        Future Value Page:  
    </p>
    <p>
        FutureValueInputs.razor - This displays the input fields for future value inputs. The parent component passes in a FutureValue object as a parameter. This object will hold the future value data input into the form. The component also has an EventCallback called OnValidSubmit. When the user submits the form, a method is called that then invokes whatever method is assigned to it by the parent component.  
    </p>
    <p>
        FutureValueResults.razor - This just displays a message. That message is passed in by the parent component as a parameter. This component has no event callbacks, the data only passes one way.  
    </p>
    <p>
        Balance Sheet Page:    
    </p>
    <p>
        BalanceSheetList.razor - This displays the list of assets and liabilities from the balance sheet, their totals, and total equity. The parent component passes in the balance sheet object as well as equity as a parameter. There are four event callbacks. OnDeleteAsset is tied to a parent method to delete a given asset. OnDeleteLiability is the same but for a liability. They both also pass a specific line item back to the parent representing the line item to be deleted. OnAddAsset and OnAddLiability are tied to respective parent methods to add an asset or a liability.  
    </p>
    <p>
        BalanceSheetItemDialog.razor - This displays the input form to add a line item (asset or liability) to the balance sheet. It receives a line item as a parameter. There are two event callbacks - OnValidSubmit and OnCancel. They are tied to their respective parent methods to either add the line item or cancel.  
    </p>
    <p>
        Reserve Study Page:   
    </p>
    <p>
        ReserveStudyFilters.razor - This displays the non-component values of the reserve study (Study Horizon, a fixed value, and Number of Units, which the user can edit). The reserve study object is passed in as a parameter. There is an editform that is shown if the user clicks edit next to number of units. There is an event callback tied to a method that just causes the parent component to re-render, updating study calculations if they have changed.
    </p>
    <p>
        ReserveItemList.razor - This displays the list of components in the reserve study. The study is passed in as a parameter. There are two event callbacks: OnDeleteComponent and OnAddComponent. OnDeleteComponent is tied to a parent method to delete a component and it passes the component to be deleted. OnAddComponent also calls a parent method to initiate adding a new component.  
    </p>
    <p>
        ReserveItemDialog.razor - This displays an edit form to add a new component to the list of components. The model component is passed in by the parent. There is an event callback that is tied to a parent method to add a new component.  
    </p>
    <p>
        YearlyTotalsChart.razor - This displays the line graph tracking annual expenses. The study is passed in as a parameter from the parent. There is no event callback as no data is sent back to the parent. Whenever parameters are set, annual expenditures are calculated and a redraw of the chart is triggered. No chart is displayed if there are no items in the components list. Note: The assignment labels the the MonthlyTotalsChart, but the previous assignment had us doing annual expenditures, not monthly, so I will stick with that.  
    </p>
    <p>
        AI Usage: ChatGPT was used in the debugging process and to help with ideation. It did not contribute meaningfully to the code itself, but it did save me time debugging. 
    </p>
</div>

<h3>P2 README:</h3>

<div>
    <p>
        Project Overview:  
        In the first part of this project, I built a Future Value Calculator Page. This update adds a Balance Sheet Page and Reserve Study Page. I will go over the new features, and post the old README files on the about page for consultation. The first page added is the Balance Sheet. It contains columns for Assets and Liabilities. The user can add/delete assets or liabilities from each column. The Balance Sheet will dynamically display total assets, liabilities, and equity. The user can also upload a balance sheet (not currently parsed per instructions) and save the filename and a timestamp. The user can also download the displayed balance sheet to a .docx file. The Reserve Study page contains a list of components. The user can add/delete items from this list. If there are components in the list, the Reserve Study page will dynamically create a line graph of yearly expenses over the 30 year study horizon, as well as displaying total costs over the 30 year study and a recommended monthly HOA fee per unit. The user can also change the number of units, dynamically updating calculations. Additionally, the user can import a .csv file to populate the list of components as well as exporting the current component list to a .csv file.    
    </p>
    <p>
        How to run:  
        Running the project from VS Code will start it up on a local server, which can be visited in a browser's address bar. This will take you to the home page with a navigation menu on the right. Clicking a page will take you there.  
        On the balance sheet page, you can add an asset or liability by clicking the associated buttons. You will see a form with several input fields. Fill it in and click submit, and it will be added to the list. To remove an asset or liability, click delete next to that line item. You will be asked if you are sure, click yes or no. To download the currently displayed balance sheet, click download balance sheet, and a .docx file will be created and downloaded. To upload a file, click Upload Balance Sheet. Note that you can only upload a .docx file.  
        On the reserve study page, you can add a component by clicking Add Component. You will see a form with several input fields. Fill it in and click submit, and it will be added to the list. To remove a component, click delete next to that component. You will be asked if you are sure, clikc yes or not. To download the currently displayed component list, click export to .csv file. To import a component list to be displayed, click import .csv file. Uploaded .csv files must be in a specific format to get the data from them. The first line is a header and should read "Name,UsefulLife,ReplacementCost". Each successive line contains a component in this format: "[component.Name],[component.UsefulLife],[component.ReplacementCost]". Example Line: Roof,15,10000 
    </p>
    <p>
        Home - / <br /> 
            Future Value Calculator - /future-value  <br />  
            Balance Sheet - /balance-sheet    <br />
            Reserve Study - /reserve-study <br />
    </p>
    <p>
        Component Breakdown:  
        This project uses separate page components for each page. Bootstrap components are used for forms (EditForm), data validation (DataAnnotationsValidator, ValidationSummary), data inputs (InputText, InputNumber), file upload (InputFile), and tab titles (PageTitle). Other "components" created include two new JavaScript files in wwwroot. These define functions called for downloading files from the browser and building the line chart for the reserve study.    
    </p>
    <p>
        Where State Lives:  
        Each page has its own state living in model classes. I kept the forms on their parent pages for a simpler architecture. In the future, I will use things like modals for forms that will pass state from parent to child through parameters, and back up through event callbacks.    
    </p>
    <p>
        Testing Summary:  
        I created six unit tests for each service. This is because each service ended up with three methods. Each method was tested using normal numbers and hand calculations, as well as testing the results when inputting empty lists and zero values. One method throws an error if it is going to try to divide by zero; that was tested as well.  
    </p>
    <p>
        AI Usage Disclosure:  
        ChatGPT was used throughout the project to help with ideation and scaffolding. There are very few places where I simply copy/pasted output from ChatGPT - the vast majority of the code is my own. The places where I utilized ChatGPT the most were for file upload/download and creating a line graph, as neither of these have been addressed in the classroom. In this case, the AI provided two javascript files for me: chart.js and download.js. The functions within create a chart or download a .docx/.csv. I also had to install the dotnet OpenXML SDK in order to write to .docx. Chat also walked me through the C# methods for downloading/writing a file. The sections with AI-produced code will be commented as such.  
    </p>
</div>

<h3>First README:</h3>

<div>
    <p>
        Project Overview:  
        The goal of this project is to make a simple Blazor web page with a Home page and navigation to a Future Value Calculator. The user should be able to input values for Principal, Interest Rate, Years, and Compounds Per Year. When the user clicks submit, these values will be stored in a model class object, and that will be used to calculate the future value and return that to the user. This project uses an EditForm, a model class, data binding, and unit testing.  
    </p>
    <p>
        How to run:  
        You should be able to run this project as is from VS Code. When you navigate to the local web address, you will see a simple home page with a navigation menu on the left side. Currently, the only other pages are "Future Value Calculator" and "About" (located at the top right). At Future Value Calculator, you can input values for Principal, Annual Interest Rate, Number of Years, and Number of Compounds Per Year. Once you have input values, click submit to see a summary of the results. At the about page, you will see my information and a copy of this README.  
    </p>
    <p>
        Page map/routing:  
        There are only three pages: A home page, a future value calculator page, and an about page.  
        The home page is located at localhost:[port]  
        The future value calculator page is located at localhost:[port]/future-value  
        The about page is located at localhost:[port]/about  
        Both main pages can be visited by clicking links in the navigation menu. The about page can be visited by clicking about in the top right corner. 
    </p>
    <p>
        Component Breakdown:  
        This project contains many default components from blazor, namely MainLayout and NavMenu. I edited NavMenu to only include the pages used in this project. I removed most of the default pages, but left Error.razor and NotFound.razor for future implementation. I also had to edit MainLayout to reroute the about page.    
    </p>
    <p>
        Where State Lives/How it Flows:  
        This all takes place in the Future Value Calculator page. When the page loads, the input fields are bound to a future value model class object which has variable for each of the input fields. When the user inputs data, it is stored in the object, which calculates the future value. Then, when the user clicks submit, the message variable is changed to reflect the user inputs and the calculated result.  
    </p>
    <p>
        Testing Summary:  
        I tested the calculations with standard inputs, as well as zero values and negative values for each variable. Input validation is in place to prevent these values, but the class methods handle these edge cases as well.  
    </p>
    <p>
        AI Usage Disclosure (and other sources):  
        ChatGPT was used sparingly when I got stuck debugging or wanted to understand a concept better. It didn't produce any of my code or any other output you see. I got the accounting stock photo from https://www.istockphoto.com/photos/accounting. 
    </p>
</div>